{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,238p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | nl -ba\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# yin.vm.linker: the universal code linker over dao.stream\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design, proposed (2026-09-24, revision r2). Supersedes the\n     4\tnamespace placement and the two-backend scope of\n     5\t`yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` (Phase B6); that document's fetch pipeline,\n     6\tformat records, refusal vocabulary, and same-root pairing are carried\n     7\there in substance and extended to all four `yin.vm` execution backends.\n     8\tRevision r2 reconciles the adversarial review\n     9\t`collab/1790250614225-reviewer-universal-linker-spec.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md`;\n    10\tsection 13 records each finding and its resolution. Subordinate to\n    11\t`datom.world.md`, `dao.stream.md`, and `dao.jing.md`. Builds on the code\n    12\tidentity rules of `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (UCF) section\n    13\t7.3, the addressing and derivation rules of `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`\n    14\tsections 4 and 5, and the ledger of `yin.vm.ledger`.\n    15\t\n    16\tRelated documents:\n    17\t\n    18\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` - the B6 predecessor (two\n    19\t  formats, one synchronous fetch)\n    20\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` - governing decisions D5 to D16\n    21\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` - AST rows, segment vectors,\n    22\t  derivation records\n    23\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.dependency-completion.md` - the work-item fixed\n    24\t  point that computes a program's requirements\n    25\t- `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` - code identity,\n    26\t  contract stamp, safepoints, pending waits, primitive profiles\n    27\t- `docs/design/dao.jing.md` - content-addressed storage and the stepped\n    28\t  remote client\n    29\t- `docs/design/dao.stream.md` - the stream contract, in particular OD-5\n    30\t\n    31\t## 1. Objective and architectural position\n    32\t\n    33\t`yin.vm` has four execution backends, all reachable from `yin.repl`\n    34\t(`src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc`, `vm-constructors`):\n    35\t\n    36\t| Backend       | Kernel                    | Code shape                   |\n    37\t|---------------|---------------------------|------------------------------|\n    38\t| `:ast-walker` | `yin.vm.ast-walker`       | universal AST row set        |\n    39\t| `:semantic`   | `yin.vm.semantic`         | positional instruction vector|\n    40\t| `:stack`      | `yin.vm.debruijn.stack`   | de Bruijn stack image (H)    |\n    41\t| `:register`   | `yin.vm.debruijn.register`| de Bruijn register image (R) |\n    42\t\n    43\tEach backend needs the same service: given an identity for some code, and\n    44\tnothing else, obtain the code, prove it is the code the identity names,\n    45\tprove it is well formed, prove the receiver can bind its dependencies\n    46\tidentically to the publisher, and hand it over for loading. Today that\n    47\tservice exists once, for two of the four backends, under a name that says\n    48\tit belongs to one of them: `yin.vm.debruijn-linker`.\n    49\t\n    50\tThis document makes two corrections and one unification.\n    51\t\n    52\t**Correction 1: the linker is universal.** The B6 fetch function has no\n    53\tde Bruijn branch in it. It is parameterized by a format record and runs\n    54\tthe same six steps for a stack image and a register image. The namespace\n    55\t`yin.vm.debruijn-linker` is therefore a misnomer that invites a second\n    56\tlinker for the semantic VM and a third for the walker. There is one\n    57\tlinker, `yin.vm.linker`, and every backend is a format record it takes.\n    58\t\n    59\t**Correction 2: local and remote linking are one mechanism.** B6 already\n    60\tstates this (`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` section 7.2: \"the boundary is\n    61\t`dao.stream`; local and remote resolution are the same transport-neutral\n    62\tmechanism\"). But `fetch` today reads through a synchronous `dao.jing`\n    63\thandle, and a local composition hands it an in-memory store whose `get` is\n    64\ta map lookup. That is a function call across what the axioms say is a\n    65\tstream boundary. This document makes the request and response a stream\n    66\texchange in every composition; what varies is the transport underneath\n    67\t(ring buffer, file, WebSocket, DHT behind a served boundary), never the\n    68\tlinker's shape.\n    69\t\n    70\t**Unification: `require` is linking.** Module loading in `yin.vm.module`\n    71\tresolves `(require 'foo)` against a dictionary the composition built by\n    72\thand at wiring time. Linking resolves an identity to content over a stream.\n    73\tThese are the same operation at two grains: a module name is an entry in a\n    74\tname environment whose value is a manifest address, and a manifest names\n    75\tthe images the linker fetches. `(require 'foo)` lowers to an effect that\n    76\tparks the VM and emits a link request; the module arrives as verified\n    77\tcontent on a response stream and is installed by a child evaluation the\n    78\tscheduler owns. The dictionary becomes a manifest registry: a value naming\n    79\tcontent addresses, never holding code that arrived by linking.\n    80\t\n    81\t### 1.1 What this document adds over B6\n    82\t\n    83\t1. The namespace `yin.vm.linker` and its migration from\n    84\t   `yin.vm.debruijn-linker` (section 9).\n    85\t2. Two new format records, `:yin.ast/code` and `:yin.semantic/code`, beside\n    86\t   the two B6 records (section 5).\n    87\t3. A `:parts-fn` slot in the format record so that a multi-row payload\n    88\t   (an AST tree) fetches through the same pipeline as a single vector,\n    89\t   with row-local validation and composition-supplied bounds before any\n    90\t   child is enqueued (section 4.2, step 2).\n    91\t4. Identity-directed matching: `:identity-matches-fn` replaces a bare\n    92\t   hash comparison, so storage-derived identities verify under the\n    93\t   algorithm the identity carries (section 4.1).\n    94\t5. Dependency obligations `{name kind expected}` replacing bare free\n    95\t   names, discharged by exact profile or manifest equality at the\n    96\t   receiver (section 4.2, step 5).\n    97\t6. The stepped link interface (`link-state`, `request-link`, `step`,\n    98\t   `abandon`) as the portable shape, with a plain-data wire request and\n    99\t   all indexes and capabilities held in linker-local state; the\n   100\t   synchronous `fetch` is reclassified as host policy over an explicit\n   101\t   link runtime (section 6).\n   102\t7. The `:module/require` effect lowered onto the linker: two wait\n   103\t   states, cursor-before-append, exact id correlation, and installation\n   104\t   as a scheduler child task (section 7).\n   105\t8. The module manifest, pinned to one canonical tree by content-addressed\n   106\t   derivation records, and the manifest registry that replaces the\n   107\t   in-memory module dictionary (section 8).\n   108\t9. A fail-closed name-authority policy for `dao.space` assertions,\n   109\t   an entry criterion for M4 (section 8.2).\n   110\t10. Eight additional refusals (section 4.3).\n   111\t\n   112\t### 1.2 What this document does not add\n   113\t\n   114\t- No transport. `dao.stream` and its transports are the only medium.\n   115\t- No peer routing, caching, or responder. `dao.jing.remote` serves\n   116\t  content; `dao.jing.dht` finds it, behind that served boundary.\n   117\t- No global registry, loader, or callback. Every handle, index, stream,\n   118\t  and format record is an explicit argument or a value in VM state.\n   119\t- No execution inside the linker. The linker fetches and verifies. Loading\n   120\t  an image into a kernel and running a module body belong to the VM's\n   121\t  scheduler.\n   122\t- No change to `image-hash`, `register-hash`, `segment-key`, the opcode\n   123\t  tables, or any kernel's step function.\n   124\t\n   125\t## 2. Governing invariants\n   126\t\n   127\tEvery rule below derives from `datom.world.md`. The derivations are named\n   128\tonce here so later sections can cite them.\n   129\t\n   130\t**I1. Code at rest is content in `dao.jing`.** An image, a row, a vector,\n   131\ta manifest, a derivation record: each is an opaque value at its\n   132\t`segment-key`. `dao.jing` assigns identity and retrieval and nothing else\n   133\t(`dao.jing.md`, *Definition*). The linker never asks the store what a\n   134\tvalue means.\n   135\t\n   136\t**I2. Code in motion is a stream in `dao.stream`.** A request for code, a\n   137\tpayload answering it, a refusal, an absence, a timeout: each is a value\n   138\tat a position in an append-only sequence. No function call crosses a host\n   139\tboundary (`datom.world.md`, *Host Boundaries*). Consequently every value\n   140\ton the link or content streams is plain data that survives the host\n   141\tcodec unchanged: no function, handle, or ambient state (`dao.stream.md`,\n   142\t*Envelopes*).\n   143\t\n   144\t**I3. Location is not identity.** Whether the content is in this process,\n   145\tin a file, behind a socket, or on a DHT peer changes how bytes are\n   146\tobtained, not what the linker checks (`dao.jing.md`, *Reads*). A local\n   147\tcomposition and a remote one differ in the creation specification of the\n   148\tstreams they wire, and in nothing the linker can observe.\n   149\t\n   150\t**I4. No operation waits.** The portable linker interface is a step that\n   151\treturns what is true now, including \"not yet\" (`dao.stream.md`, OD-5). A\n   152\twaiting `fetch` may wrap the step as host policy on hosts that can afford\n   153\tit and is never the interface another interpreter is written against.\n   154\t\n   155\t**I5. Verification is address-directed and identity-directed, and both\n   156\trun.** The storage address and the format identity have different\n   157\tpreimages for two of the four formats and the same preimage for the other\n   158\ttwo; in every case both checks execute, because an index entry is a claim\n   159\tand never a proof (`yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md` section 6; D14). Every\n   160\tcheck is directed by the algorithm the address or identity carries; the\n   161\tlinker never re-mints under a default and compares with `=` (Class 3 of\n   162\t`dao.jing.call-site-classification.md`).\n   163\t\n   164\t**I6. The VM never invokes a loader.** Absence of code is a park plus a\n   165\trequest emission; the loaded image arrives as a value in VM state\n   166\t(`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` section 7.2). The linker is an observer on a\n   167\trequest stream, not a function the kernel calls.\n   168\t\n   169\t**I7. The registry is a value.** `yin.vm.module` already keeps the\n   170\tregistry as a map in VM state. This document keeps that and changes what\n   171\tthe map holds: manifests, derivation records, and bindings produced by\n   172\tan evaluation the scheduler ran, never code that arrived on a stream.\n   173\t\n   174\t**I8. Fail closed.** Every ambiguity the linker can detect (two manifests\n   175\tfor one name, an image whose derivation does not lead back to the\n   176\tmanifest's tree, an obligation whose profile differs) is a refusal, never\n   177\ta choice.\n   178\t\n   179\t## 3. Identities: three kinds, one address space\n   180\t\n   181\tThe linker moves between three kinds of name. Confusing them was the\n   182\tdefect B6 guarded against with D9, and the unification here adds a third.\n   183\t\n   184\t| Kind             | Example                              | Owner            |\n   185\t|------------------|--------------------------------------|------------------|\n   186\t| storage address  | `:segment/blake3-...`                | `dao.jing`       |\n   187\t| format identity  | H, R, a vector address, a root row id| the format       |\n   188\t| module name      | `'my.lib`                            | name environment |\n   189\t\n   190\t**Storage address.** `(jing/segment-key payload)` under the storage\n   191\tencoder in force. Changes when the encoder changes (the CBOR landing,\n   192\t`dao.jing.md` *Open items*). Carries its own hash algorithm.\n   193\t\n   194\t**Format identity.** What the VM contract calls the code. There are two\n   195\tfamilies:\n   196\t\n   197\t- *Contract-pinned identities* (H, R): computed by the format's own hash\n   198\t  function over a descriptor that embeds the lowering contract version,\n   199\t  pinned to SHA-256 by contract freeze, independent of the storage\n   200\t  encoder. Their preimage differs from the storage address's preimage.\n   201\t  For these two formats, storage address and VM identity are strictly\n   202\t  decoupled (D9).\n   203\t- *Storage-derived identities* (`:yin.ast/code`, `:yin.semantic/code`):\n   204\t  UCF section 7.3.2 defines a segment's `:yin.code/hash` as the\n   205\t  `segment-key` of its canonical instruction vector, and\n   206\t  `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 4.1 defines a tree's address as its\n   207\t  root row's id, itself a `segment-key`. Here the identity's preimage is\n   208\t  the payload, so the identity is a storage address, carries a hash\n   209\t  algorithm of its own, and changes when the storage encoder changes.\n   210\t  **Strict decoupling therefore holds only for H and R today.** UCF\n   211\t  accepts this (\"UCF must not mint a second addressing scheme\"); this\n   212\t  document inherits it, verifies these identities under the algorithm\n   213\t  they carry (section 4.1), and records the amendment that would pin\n   214\t  them as a UCF and code-as-tuples decision, not a linker one (section\n   215\t  12).\n   216\t\n   217\t**Module name.** A symbol resolved through a name environment to a\n   218\tmanifest address (section 8). A name is a claim of whoever asserted it;\n   219\tthe manifest it resolves to is content and is verified like any other,\n   220\tincluding that it declares the name it was resolved under.\n   221\t\n   222\tThe relation among them, for one module and one backend:\n   223\t\n   224\t```text\n   225\tname 'my.lib\n   226\t  --(name environment, one accepted assertion)--> manifest address\n   227\t  --(fetch, verify, name check)-->                manifest value\n   228\t  --(:yin.module/tree)-->                         canonical tree address\n   229\t  --(:yin.module/derivations, format f)-->        derivation record\n   230\t  --(record :yin.ledger/output)-->                format identity\n   231\t  --(format index)-->                             storage address\n   232\t  --(fetch, verify)-->                            image value\n   233\t```\n   234\t\n   235\tTwo indexes appear: the name environment (name to manifest address) and\n   236\tthe format index (identity to storage address). Both are linker-local\n   237\tstate (section 6.2); neither ever travels on the link stream.\n   238\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
