{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '547,701p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | nl -ba -v 547\"","aggregated_output":"   547\t## 6. Linking is a stream exchange\n   548\t\n   549\t### 6.1 The topology\n   550\t\n   551\t```mermaid\n   552\tflowchart LR\n   553\t  subgraph VM[\"yin.vm scheduler (any backend)\"]\n   554\t    K[park on :link wait entry]\n   555\t  end\n   556\t  subgraph LNK[\"yin.vm.linker interpreter\"]\n   557\t    L[link-state / step]\n   558\t  end\n   559\t  subgraph SRV[\"content server boundary\"]\n   560\t    J[serve-content! over a jing handle]\n   561\t    D[(mem / file / dht handle)]\n   562\t    J --- D\n   563\t  end\n   564\t  K -- \"link request stream\" --> L\n   565\t  L -- \"link response stream\" --> K\n   566\t  L -- \"content request stream\" --> J\n   567\t  J -- \"content response stream\" --> L\n   568\t```\n   569\t\n   570\tFour streams, two pairs. The VM and the linker share the link pair; the\n   571\tlinker and the content server share the content pair. Each pair is\n   572\twired by the composition, and its transport is the composition's choice:\n   573\t\n   574\t| Composition           | Link pair    | Content pair    | Behind server |\n   575\t|-----------------------|--------------|-----------------|---------------|\n   576\t| single process, tests | ring buffer  | ring buffer     | mem handle    |\n   577\t| durable local         | ring buffer  | ring buffer     | file handle   |\n   578\t| remote content        | ring buffer  | ws + rpc        | remote's      |\n   579\t| peer network          | ring buffer  | ring buffer     | dht handle    |\n   580\t\n   581\tNothing in the linker or the VM changes across rows. This is I3 made\n   582\toperational: a \"local\" require and a \"remote\" require are the same four\n   583\tappends and four reads.\n   584\t\n   585\tThe content pair is `dao.jing.remote`'s existing wire vocabulary:\n   586\t`:jing/get-content` answering `{:found? boolean :value v}` over\n   587\t`dao.stream.rpc`. The linker writes no protocol of its own for content.\n   588\t\n   589\t**The DHT sits behind the served boundary.** `dao.jing.dht`'s handle is\n   590\ta synchronous `dao.jing` handle over `IDhtNet`, not a stepped DaoStream\n   591\tendpoint. It is not made one here. It is placed where the file and memory\n   592\thandles are: behind `serve-content!`, whose handler calls the handle. The\n   593\tlinker side is unchanged, and the server side is driven by whoever drives\n   594\tthe server today. A portable stepped DHT client would be a `dao.jing.dht`\n   595\tdesign and is out of scope; the peer-network row is therefore equivalent\n   596\tat the linker's boundary and no further, and M3's tests say so (section\n   597\t9).\n   598\t\n   599\t### 6.2 Linker-local state\n   600\t\n   601\t`link-state` is constructed once by the composition with everything the\n   602\tlinker needs to answer requests, none of which travels on a stream:\n   603\t\n   604\t```clojure\n   605\t(link-state {:rpc          rpc-state          ; dao.stream.rpc client\n   606\t             :formats      {format record}    ; section 5\n   607\t             :indexes      {format index}     ; identity -> address\n   608\t             :name-env     name-env           ; section 8.2, at snapshot\n   609\t             :authority    {...}              ; section 8.2\n   610\t             :bounds       {:max-parts n :max-depth d :max-bytes b}\n   611\t             :fallback     :none | :trusted | :verifying})\n   612\t```\n   613\t\n   614\tFunctions (`:formats`, an index given as a function) live here and only\n   615\there. A composition that wants a remote name environment or index\n   616\tobserves it into this state through an ordinary `dao.space` read over a\n   617\tstream the linker holds a cursor on; step 1 consults only what has been\n   618\tobserved.\n   619\t\n   620\t### 6.3 The stepped interface\n   621\t\n   622\tFollowing `dao.stream.md` OD-5 and the shape `dao.jing.remote.step`\n   623\talready has, the portable linker is a pure function over explicit state.\n   624\t\n   625\t```clojure\n   626\t(link-state opts)               ; -> state; no socket, atom, or scheduler\n   627\t(request-link state request)    ; -> [state link-id]\n   628\t(step state budget)             ; -> {:state s :completions [...]\n   629\t                                ;     :diagnostics [...]}\n   630\t(abandon state link-id reason)  ; -> state\n   631\t```\n   632\t\n   633\tA `request` is plain data and nothing else. Its closed key set:\n   634\t\n   635\t```clojure\n   636\t{:yin.link/id       7                        ; caller-minted, never reused\n   637\t :yin.link/format   :yin.debruijn.code\n   638\t :yin.link/contract \"b1\"                     ; optional; step 4\n   639\t :yin.link/name     'my.lib                  ; exactly one of :name,\n   640\t :yin.link/identity H}                       ;   :identity is present\n   641\t```\n   642\t\n   643\tA request carries no index, no receiver, no function, and no handle;\n   644\ta request with any key outside this set, or with a value that is not a\n   645\tscalar, keyword, symbol, or address, is refused `:invalid-request` on\n   646\tthe response stream and never processed. The receiver's capabilities do\n   647\tnot travel because discharge happens at the receiver (section 4.2, 5b).\n   648\t\n   649\t`step` advances every in-flight link in a fixed order: re-attempt\n   650\tunsent content requests, poll the content response medium at most\n   651\t`budget` elements, route each correlated response to its link, run the\n   652\tverification step that response enables, issue the next content request\n   653\tthe worklist needs (a part, under `:parts-fn`), and take completions.\n   654\tEach link is a small state machine over the six steps; steps 3 to 5a\n   655\tnever touch a stream and run the moment step 2 completes.\n   656\t\n   657\tA completion is the step-6 result or a refusal, tagged with its link id:\n   658\t\n   659\t```clojure\n   660\t{:yin.link/id 7 :status :ok :manifest m :derivation d :image {...}}\n   661\t{:yin.link/id 7 :status :refused :reason :absent :address a}\n   662\t```\n   663\t\n   664\tA link with no completion yet is `:pending`; the caller steps again\n   665\twhen it chooses. Retry cadence, deadlines, and permanent-absence policy\n   666\tare the composition's, as D6 states: streams report, they do not decide.\n   667\t\n   668\t`abandon` retires a link's bookkeeping and completes it `:lost` with the\n   669\tgiven reason, so a caller that gives up still receives exactly one\n   670\tcompletion and never reuses the id.\n   671\t\n   672\t### 6.4 `fetch` is host policy over an explicit runtime\n   673\t\n   674\tThe B6 signature changes in its first argument:\n   675\t\n   676\t```clojure\n   677\t(fetch runtime format identity)\n   678\t(fetch runtime format identity receiver)\n   679\t```\n   680\t\n   681\t`runtime` is `{:state link-state :drive (fn [state] state')}`. `:drive`\n   682\tis the composition's driver: it steps the linker's client side *and*\n   683\twhatever serves the content pair (`dao.stream.rpc/serve-once!` over the\n   684\tcontent handle, or nothing when a remote server runs elsewhere), and it\n   685\tis called in a loop until the one link completes. `fetch` therefore\n   686\tholds no `dao.jing` handle and cannot call `jing/get`: the content\n   687\thandle is reachable only from the server side of the content pair. A\n   688\ttest in M3 asserts this by wiring a server whose handle counts `get`\n   689\tcalls and a linker state with no handle at all, and by checking that\n   690\tevery local `fetch` produces exactly the request/response traffic on the\n   691\tcontent pair that the stepped path produces. `fetch` runs step 5b\n   692\tagainst the `receiver` argument before returning.\n   693\t\n   694\t`verify`, the pure steps 3 to 5a over values already in hand, and\n   695\t`discharge`, the pure step 5b over obligations and a receiver, are both\n   696\texported so tests and compositions holding a payload can run the checks\n   697\twithout a stream.\n   698\t\n   699\tNo new interpreter may be written against `fetch`. The VM path of\n   700\tsection 7 is written against `step`.\n   701\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '702,917p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | nl -ba -v 702\"","aggregated_output":"   702\t## 7. `(require 'foo)` lowers to the linker\n   703\t\n   704\t### 7.1 Today\n   705\t\n   706\t`yin.vm/primitives` binds `require` to an effectful primitive that\n   707\treturns `{:effect :module/require :module ns-sym}`.\n   708\t`yin.vm.engine/handle-effect` dispatches it to the registry's effect\n   709\thandler, and `yin.vm.module/require-handler` answers from\n   710\t`(:modules state)` alone: present, the module name; absent, a thrown\n   711\t`ex-info`. The registry was populated by `register-module` at wiring\n   712\ttime with host functions. This is the \"static ad-hoc in-memory\n   713\tdictionary lookup\" the critique names. Its two defects: a module that is\n   714\tnot in the dictionary cannot be obtained at all, and the dictionary holds\n   715\tcode (host functions) rather than naming it.\n   716\t\n   717\t### 7.2 The unified flow\n   718\t\n   719\t```mermaid\n   720\tsequenceDiagram\n   721\t  participant P as program\n   722\t  participant S as VM scheduler\n   723\t  participant L as yin.vm.linker\n   724\t  participant J as content server (over dao.stream)\n   725\t  P->>S: (require 'foo)\n   726\t  S->>S: :module/require effect\n   727\t  alt manifest already linked in registry value\n   728\t    S-->>P: 'foo\n   729\t  else not linked\n   730\t    S->>S: mint response cursor; :link-request entry\n   731\t    S->>L: append link request {id, name 'foo, format f}\n   732\t    S->>S: append ok -> :link-response entry\n   733\t    L->>L: name env at snapshot: 'foo -> one manifest\n   734\t    L->>J: get-content manifest address\n   735\t    J-->>L: manifest (verified; name checked)\n   736\t    L->>J: get-content derivation record, image(s)\n   737\t    J-->>L: image (verified, 6 steps; derivation checked)\n   738\t    L->>S: append link response {id, :ok image, obligations}\n   739\t    S->>S: match id; discharge obligations (5b)\n   740\t    S->>S: child task: loading -> running -> validated\n   741\t    S->>S: registry value += {'foo manifest, bindings}\n   742\t    S-->>P: 'foo\n   743\t  end\n   744\t```\n   745\t\n   746\tThe steps, as rules:\n   747\t\n   748\t1. **The effect is unchanged.** `require` still returns\n   749\t   `{:effect :module/require :module 'foo}`. Nothing upstream of the\n   750\t   engine learns anything.\n   751\t2. **The handler checks the registry value first.** A manifest already\n   752\t   linked in `(:modules state)` answers now, as `next` answers `ok` when\n   753\t   the value is there. This is not the dictionary lookup surviving: the\n   754\t   registry holds manifests and bindings produced by an earlier child\n   755\t   evaluation, or by the composition's host-module declaration (section\n   756\t   8.3). A module currently installing (section 7.3) is neither linked\n   757\t   nor absent: the requiring task joins that install's waiter set.\n   758\t3. **A miss mints a cursor, then parks in the request state.** In this\n   759\t   order: the handler mints a `:dao.stream/newest` cursor on the link\n   760\t   response stream; then it builds the wait entry in its first state:\n   761\t\n   762\t   ```clojure\n   763\t   {:reason    :link-request\n   764\t    :link-id   7                       ; from the engine's gensym\n   765\t    :envelope  {:yin.link/id 7 :yin.link/name 'foo\n   766\t                :yin.link/format :yin.debruijn.code\n   767\t                :yin.link/contract \"b1\"}\n   768\t    :request   {:dao.stream/identity ... :dao.stream/descriptor ...}\n   769\t    :response  {:dao.stream/identity ... :dao.stream/descriptor ...}\n   770\t    :cursor    <the kept cursor minted above>}\n   771\t   ```\n   772\t\n   773\t   Minting before appending is the `dao.stream.md` *Cursors* rule for\n   774\t   observing events caused by an operation: a response that lands\n   775\t   before the cursor exists would otherwise be skipped. The entry is\n   776\t   registers, resource ids, and plain data only; no handle and no\n   777\t   closure is attached.\n   778\t4. **The request is appended; `ok` moves the entry to the response\n   779\t   state.** The handler appends `:envelope` through the link request\n   780\t   writer the composition supplied in `opts`. On `ok` the entry becomes\n   781\t   `{:reason :link-response :link-id 7 :response ... :cursor ...}`. On\n   782\t   `full` the entry stays in `:link-request` with its envelope retained\n   783\t   verbatim and is retried on the next poll, the `:ffi-request`\n   784\t   discipline. The two states are the two UCF pending variants this\n   785\t   flow adds (section 11, item 10): `:link-request` carries everything\n   786\t   needed to rebuild the envelope, `:link-response` carries the response\n   787\t   descriptor and the kept cell.\n   788\t5. **The linker interpreter observes the request stream** with its own\n   789\t   cursor, in its own control flow. It resolves the name through its\n   790\t   name environment at the snapshot it was composed with (section 8.2),\n   791\t   fetches the manifest by address through the `:yin.module/manifest`\n   792\t   format record so the manifest itself passes steps 2 to 4, checks the\n   793\t   manifest's declared name (section 8.1), selects the derivation and\n   794\t   identity for the requested format, verifies the derivation, and runs\n   795\t   the six steps for the image through the stepped core. A manifest with\n   796\t   no derivation for the requested format is `:unsupported-format`, and\n   797\t   the composed `:fallback` policy applies before a refusal is appended.\n   798\t6. **The response is appended** to the link response stream with the\n   799\t   request's exact `:yin.link/id`, as `:ok` with the image, manifest,\n   800\t   derivation, and obligations, or as the refusal. The linker holds\n   801\t   nothing afterwards.\n   802\t7. **`check-wait-set` polls each `:link-response` entry's kept cursor and\n   803\t   restores only on exact correlation.** On each poll the entry advances\n   804\t   past every response whose `:yin.link/id` is not its own, keeping its\n   805\t   cursor at the first unconsumed position, and acts only on a response\n   806\t   whose id equals `:link-id`. This is the per-cell round-order\n   807\t   discipline UCF section 7.5.3 already gives shared cursors, so a\n   808\t   parked `:link-response` lifts and lowers as any other wait does.\n   809\t   Handling of every other case is fixed:\n   810\t   - *duplicate*: a second response for an id whose entry has already\n   811\t     restored is skipped; ids are minted once from the engine counter\n   812\t     and never reused, so the entry cannot exist again;\n   813\t   - *late*: a response for an id whose entry was abandoned is skipped;\n   814\t   - *unknown*: a response for an id no entry holds is skipped;\n   815\t   - *abandoned*: a composition that gives up on a link removes the\n   816\t     entry, calls `abandon` on the linker side, and raises the reason to\n   817\t     the program as the effect's error; the id is retired.\n   818\t   Each skip emits a telemetry diagnostic and consumes nothing but the\n   819\t   cursor position. On a matching `:ok` the scheduler runs step 5b\n   820\t   against its live state and, if discharged, starts the install child\n   821\t   (section 7.3). On a matching refusal, or a failed discharge, the\n   822\t   program resumes with the refusal raised as the effect's error, the\n   823\t   same way an `:ffi` `error` raises. On `gap` the honest outcome is a\n   824\t   `gap`, and the composition's policy decides.\n   825\t\n   826\tRevision r1 claimed steps 3 to 7 needed no new engine machinery. That\n   827\twas wrong in two places: the wait entry needs the two-state shape above,\n   828\tand installation needs the child task below. Park entries, kept cursors,\n   829\t`handle-stream-block`, and `check-wait-set` are reused; `:link-request`,\n   830\t`:link-response`, and the install child are new.\n   831\t\n   832\t### 7.3 Installing a module is a scheduler child task\n   833\t\n   834\tThe linker delivers a verified image with its obligations. Installing it\n   835\tis the scheduler's, as an explicit child evaluation with its own state:\n   836\t\n   837\t```mermaid\n   838\tstateDiagram-v2\n   839\t  [*] --> loading: 5b discharged\n   840\t  loading --> running: kernel loader accepted the image\n   841\t  loading --> refused: loader defect\n   842\t  running --> parked: child effect blocks (stream, ffi, require)\n   843\t  parked --> running: child wait satisfied\n   844\t  running --> validated: halt; every export bound in child store\n   845\t  running --> refused: halt; :export-missing, or error\n   846\t  validated --> linked: bindings published to registry value\n   847\t  linked --> [*]\n   848\t  refused --> [*]\n   849\t```\n   850\t\n   851\tRules:\n   852\t\n   853\t- **The child is a task, not a call.** The scheduler holds\n   854\t  `:installs {name {:phase p :vm child :waiters [task-ids] :parent\n   855\t  link-id}}`. The child is a VM of the same backend with a fresh,\n   856\t  isolated store containing nothing of any other task's state (UCF\n   857\t  section 7.6.2 applied to loading), its own wait-set, and the parent's\n   858\t  registry value as its module view. The scheduler steps it in its\n   859\t  ordinary round beside every other task; nothing about it runs inside\n   860\t  wait restoration.\n   861\t- **The requiring task waits on the install, not on the linker.** Once\n   862\t  the child starts, the parent's entry becomes\n   863\t  `{:reason :install :name 'foo}`, and any other task that requires\n   864\t  `'foo` meanwhile joins `:waiters`. No task is restored until the\n   865\t  install reaches `linked` or `refused`.\n   866\t- **`running` may park.** A module body that reads a stream, calls FFI,\n   867\t  or requires another module parks the child exactly as any task parks;\n   868\t  a transitive require emits its own link request under its own id\n   869\t  (section 7.4). The parent is parked on `:install` and is unaffected;\n   870\t  the scheduler keeps stepping every other task. There is nothing to\n   871\t  deadlock on except a cycle, which section 7.4 refuses.\n   872\t- **`validated` requires halt plus exports.** The child must reach halt\n   873\t  (not error, not an abandoned park), and every name under\n   874\t  `:yin.module/exports` must be a key of the child's store. A missing\n   875\t  export is `:export-missing`; an error is the error. Nothing from a\n   876\t  refused child's store is published.\n   877\t- **`linked` is a registry update, not a code copy.** The registry\n   878\t  value gains `{'foo {:manifest m :address a :derivation d\n   879\t  :bindings slice}}` where `slice` is the child's store restricted to\n   880\t  the exports. `resolve-var`'s module step reads `:bindings`. The\n   881\t  image stays where the child's kernel loaded it; the parent kernel\n   882\t  loads it by the same verified value when a binding is first applied,\n   883\t  through its ordinary loader (a closure's segment or body pc names the\n   884\t  image by identity, and the per-stamp index resolves it). Every waiter\n   885\t  is restored with `'foo` as its value.\n   886\t- **`refused` restores every waiter with the refusal** as the effect's\n   887\t  error, and the install entry is removed so a later require may try\n   888\t  again.\n   889\t\n   890\tThe alternative, per-format export locators in the manifest (a body pc\n   891\tfor stack images, a segment and pc for semantic ones), was considered\n   892\tand rejected: it persists four descriptions of a fact that one\n   893\tevaluation derives, and it would make the manifest's shape depend on\n   894\twhich backends exist. This is *derive, don't persist* at the module\n   895\tgrain.\n   896\t\n   897\t### 7.4 Transitive requires and cycles\n   898\t\n   899\tA module body may itself `(require 'bar)`. Under section 7.3 that is the\n   900\tchild parking on its own `:link-request`, and it needs no further rule to\n   901\tcomplete. One rule prevents deadlock: before starting a child for\n   902\t`'bar`, the scheduler walks the install ancestry of the requiring task\n   903\t(the chain of `:parent` link ids through `:installs`); if `'bar` is\n   904\tinstalling anywhere on that chain, the require is `:require-cycle`\n   905\tnaming the chain, and the child that asked is refused. A cycle is legal\n   906\tonly when delivered as one unit by the linker's dependency closure.\n   907\t\n   908\tThe linker may resolve `:yin.module/requires` ahead of time from the\n   909\tmanifest and deliver every image of the closure in one response, ordered\n   910\tso that dependencies precede dependents and a strongly connected\n   911\tcomponent arrives as one unit. This is Phase B7's dependency closure\n   912\t(`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` D5, D6); this document fixes only that the\n   913\tresponse is a vector of `[manifest derivation image obligations]`\n   914\tentries in install order, that the scheduler starts one child per entry\n   915\tin that order, and that a cycle is one unit hashed with canonical\n   916\tmember ordering. How SCC identity is minted stays open (section 12).\n   917\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '310,385p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | nl -ba -v 310\"","aggregated_output":"   310\t\n   311\t1. **Resolve identity to storage address.** The index is linker-local\n   312\t   composition data: a map, or datoms `[identity attribute address]`\n   313\t   read through `index-from-datoms` from a `dao.space` source the linker\n   314\t   observes with its own cursor. An entry that has not been observed\n   315\t   yet is `:absent` now, which is the honest answer at this moment; a\n   316\t   later request may find it. Step 1 never waits and never queries a\n   317\t   remote index inline. For the storage-derived formats the identity is\n   318\t   its own address and the index is the identity function.\n   319\t2. **Fetch payload over `dao.stream`.** Each address is a\n   320\t   `:jing/get-content` request on the content request stream and a\n   321\t   correlated response on the content response stream (section 6). The\n   322\t   value is verified against the address it was requested at, using the\n   323\t   algorithm the address carries (`jing/segment-matches?`). Then, before\n   324\t   any child is enqueued, `:row-defect-fn` validates this part alone;\n   325\t   a malformed row is refused here and its slots are never followed.\n   326\t   Then `:parts-fn` names further addresses; each is fetched and\n   327\t   verified the same way; the worklist is breadth-first from the root\n   328\t   and visits each address once. The composition supplies bounds in the\n   329\t   runtime: `:max-parts`, `:max-depth`, `:max-bytes`; exceeding any is\n   330\t   `:parts-limit` naming the bound and the address at which it was hit.\n   331\t   The result of step 2 is the root value plus, for a multi-part\n   332\t   format, the map of every fetched part by address.\n   333\t3. **Verify format identity.** `(:identity-matches-fn format)` over the\n   334\t   requested identity and the root value. For H and R this is a\n   335\t   different hash under a different preimage from step 2 and catches a\n   336\t   stale or swapped index entry, a descriptor disagreement, or a\n   337\t   contract version disagreement (all `:hash-mismatch`, as in B6). For\n   338\t   the storage-derived formats it is `segment-matches?` under the\n   339\t   identity's own algorithm; it still runs, because it is the only check\n   340\t   that the *index* did not point the identity at a different, correctly\n   341\t   stored payload.\n   342\t4. **Validate structural and liveness invariants.** The format's\n   343\t   validator runs over the complete value: for a tree, over the\n   344\t   assembled row set (`:id-resolves`, `:acyclic`, `:root-reachable` are\n   345\t   whole-tree rules that step 2's row-local check cannot run); for a\n   346\t   vector, over the vector; for a register image, including the\n   347\t   ascending, bounded, exact live-set rules. A validator that throws is\n   348\t   `:descriptor-defect` with rule `:validator-refused`. If the request\n   349\t   named a contract revision and the format record's `:contract`\n   350\t   differs, the refusal is `:contract-mismatch`, raised before any\n   351\t   grammar check that would run under the wrong table.\n   352\t5. **Verify dependency closure.** Two halves.\n   353\t   - *5a, at the linker: obligations.* `:obligations-fn` yields the free\n   354\t     names. Each is joined with the manifest that delivered the image\n   355\t     (section 8.1): a name under `:yin.module/primitives` becomes\n   356\t     `{:name n :kind :primitive :profile addr}`; a name exported by a\n   357\t     module under `:yin.module/requires` becomes\n   358\t     `{:name n :kind :module :module m :manifest addr}`. A free name\n   359\t     the manifest declares nowhere is `:undeclared-free`; the publisher\n   360\t     did not say what the name means, so no receiver can bind it\n   361\t     identically. An image requested by bare identity with no manifest\n   362\t     has no declarations; it verifies only if it has no free names,\n   363\t     which is B6's closed-image case and remains supported.\n   364\t   - *5b, at the receiver: discharge.* Obligations travel with the\n   365\t     verified image in the response. The receiver checks each against\n   366\t     its live state as the first act of installation, before any load:\n   367\t     a `:primitive` obligation is discharged only by a primitive of\n   368\t     equal profile address; a `:module` obligation only by a linked\n   369\t     module of equal manifest address; a name bound in the receiver's\n   370\t     free env or store is `:shadowed-free`; anything else is\n   371\t     `:unresolved-free` naming the obligation and what was found. This\n   372\t     is UCF section 7.5.2's rule (\"checked by profile, not by presence\")\n   373\t     at the linker's grain, and D11's refusal for an image the receiver\n   374\t     cannot bind identically.\n   375\t   Discharge is at the receiver because the receiver's store and free\n   376\t   env are live state; a snapshot sent on the wire is stale by\n   377\t   construction and would license a binding that no longer holds. The\n   378\t   host-policy `fetch` (section 6.4), which runs in one process with the\n   379\t   receiver in hand, runs both halves before returning.\n   380\t6. **Return the verified image.** `{:status :ok :format f :identity i\n   381\t   :address a :value v :obligations [...]}` plus `:parts {address value}`\n   382\t   for a multi-part format and `:derivation {...}` where a manifest\n   383\t   supplied one (section 8.1). Nothing partially verified is ever\n   384\t   returned.\n   385\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"The revised text closes the obvious AST hashing error and keeps indexes and receiver capabilities off the wire. I’m checking the harder claims now: whether the manifest proves common source identity, whether response matching is safe under concurrent waits, and whether a child VM’s exported bindings can actually run in the parent."}}
