{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,230p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ledger.cljc | nl -ba\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns yin.vm.ledger\n     2\t  \"The ledger: derivation records, ledger events, and naming rows\n     3\t   (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` §5.2, §8).\n     4\t\n     5\t   The hash chain between a tree and the segment lowered from it lives\n     6\t   here, not in the code content: neither value carries the other's\n     7\t   address (§5.2), so the link is a `:derive` record — a plain map,\n     8\t   content-addressed like any other value — published as a ledger event\n     9\t   entity, with the tree and the segment named by address.\n    10\t\n    11\t   A ledger is written over a `dao.space` transactor value. Ledger\n    12\t   tx-data is dao.space tx-data (`[:db/add e a v m]` vectors, tempids in\n    13\t   `e` and `m`); `transact!` resolves it against the retained history and\n    14\t   commits the resolved datoms through the transactor, which owns\n    15\t   transaction time and appends them as one atomic record. A naming\n    16\t   datom's `m` names its event entity through the metadata-entity path\n    17\t   (§8.2): the transactor's tempid resolution replaces an `m` that is a\n    18\t   tempid with the event's allocated id.\n    19\t\n    20\t   Reading is `dao.space.query`: naming rows through the `current` view\n    21\t   and its as-of bound (§8.6), provenance walks as joins over event\n    22\t   entities, and the raw `m` of a naming row through the `history` view —\n    23\t   latest event per `[e a v]` selected with `max ?t` first, its `m`\n    24\t   interpreted second, never a hand-written negation (§8.6).\n    25\t\n    26\t   Scope: the `:derive` record and event. The `:expand` record (§8.4)\n    27\t   waits on the v2 expander, the `:publish` record on UCF custody — this\n    28\t   namespace mints neither.\"\n    29\t  (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\n    30\t            [dao.jing :as jing]\n    31\t            [dao.space.index :as index]\n    32\t            [dao.space.query :as query]\n    33\t            [dao.space.transact :as transact]\n    34\t            [dao.space.transactor :as transactor]\n    35\t            [yin.vm :as vm]\n    36\t            [yin.vm.code :as code]\n    37\t            [yin.vm.linearize :as linearize]))\n    38\t\n    39\t\n    40\t(def lowering-profile\n    41\t  \"The lowering profile this repository implements, exactly as published\n    42\t   in §5.2.1: `\\\"ast-to-bytecode\\\"` over the UCF §7.3.3 stamp\n    43\t   `{:yin.code/contract \\\"v2\\\", :yin.k/version 0}`. A `:derive` record\n    44\t   carries this map verbatim, and `verify-derivation` recomputes a\n    45\t   derivation only when the record's profile is `=` to the profile the\n    46\t   consumer implements (§5.2.2 step 2).\"\n    47\t  {:yin.lower/profile \"ast-to-bytecode\"\n    48\t   :yin.code/contract \"v2\"\n    49\t   :yin.k/version 0})\n    50\t\n    51\t\n    52\t(defn derive-record\n    53\t  \"§5.2/§8.2's `:derive` record: `output` was computed from `input` by\n    54\t   `:yin.vm/lower` under `lowering-profile`. A plain map, so it is\n    55\t   content-addressed by `dao.jing/segment-key`, immutable, and\n    56\t   verifiable; its own address is what the ledger publishes. The\n    57\t   addresses are the only coordinates — the record holds no log-local\n    58\t   id, so it is hashed once, when built.\"\n    59\t  [input output]\n    60\t  {:yin.ledger/op :derive\n    61\t   :yin.ledger/input input\n    62\t   :yin.ledger/output output\n    63\t   :yin.ledger/function :yin.vm/lower\n    64\t   :yin.ledger/profile lowering-profile})\n    65\t\n    66\t\n    67\t(defn derive-event\n    68\t  \"§8.2's ledger event tx-data for one record: a local entity with the\n    69\t   record's fields as attributes and the record's address as one more.\n    70\t   `ev` is a tempid — distinct tempids for distinct events, whether in\n    71\t   one transaction or several. The profile travels as the structured map,\n    72\t   exactly as it appears in the record; no string rendering is defined.\"\n    73\t  [ev {:yin.ledger/keys [op input output function profile] :as record}]\n    74\t  [[:db/add ev :yin.ledger/op op]\n    75\t   [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/input input]\n    76\t   [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/output output]\n    77\t   [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/function function]\n    78\t   [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/profile profile]\n    79\t   [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/record (jing/segment-key record)]])\n    80\t\n    81\t\n    82\t(defn derive-lowering\n    83\t  \"The `:derive` material for one row-lane lowering, written by\n    84\t   `linearize/lower-rows`'s caller (§5.2): `lower-rows` produces the\n    85\t   vector and provenance and knows no ledger; its caller names the two\n    86\t   addresses and mints the record. Returns the record, its address, the\n    87\t   event tx-data for tempid `ev`, and the addresses the ledger names —\n    88\t   `:tree-address` is the row set's root id, `:segment-address` the\n    89\t   vector's `(jing/segment-key vector)`.\"\n    90\t  [{:keys [root]} {:keys [vector]} ev]\n    91\t  (let [segment (jing/segment-key vector)\n    92\t        record (derive-record root segment)]\n    93\t    {:tree-address root\n    94\t     :segment-address segment\n    95\t     :record record\n    96\t     :record-address (jing/segment-key record)\n    97\t     :event (derive-event ev record)}))\n    98\t\n    99\t\n   100\t(defn name-entity\n   101\t  \"§8.3's name entity — a local entity carrying `:yin/name` — as tx-data,\n   102\t   written once when the entity is created. A later naming assertion or\n   103\t   retraction goes through `assert-name`/`retract-name` on the same\n   104\t   entity id.\"\n   105\t  [name sym]\n   106\t  [[:db/add name :yin/name sym]])\n   107\t\n   108\t\n   109\t(defn assert-name\n   110\t  \"§8.3's naming row `[t :assert tree-addr name]` as tx-data:\n   111\t   `[:db/add name :yin/code tree-addr]` on the name entity\n   112\t   (`name-entity` writes its identity datom).\n   113\t\n   114\t   With an `event` tempid shared with a `derive-event`'s tx-data, the\n   115\t   assertion's `m` resolves to the event's allocated id — the provenance\n   116\t   link from the naming datom to its event through the metadata-entity\n   117\t   path (§8.2). `naming-event` walks it back. Without one, `m` defaults\n   118\t   to the `:db/assert` marker.\"\n   119\t  ([name tree-address]\n   120\t   [[:db/add name :yin/code tree-address]])\n   121\t  ([name tree-address event]\n   122\t   [[:db/add name :yin/code tree-address event]]))\n   123\t\n   124\t\n   125\t(defn retract-name\n   126\t  \"§8.3's naming retraction `[t :retract tree-addr name]` as tx-data: the\n   127\t   reserved `:db/retract` marker in `m`. A validity op is expressed by\n   128\t   the reserved marker, never by an event (§8.2), so a retraction carries\n   129\t   no provenance link; a later assertion of the same address supersedes\n   130\t   it under the fold's latest-event-wins (§8.6).\"\n   131\t  [name tree-address]\n   132\t  [[:db/add name :yin/code tree-address (:db/retract datom/reserved)]])\n   133\t\n   134\t\n   135\t(defn transact!\n   136\t  \"Commit ledger tx-data over a `dao.space` transactor value.\n   137\t   `dao.space.transact/prepare-tx` resolves tempids — an event tempid in\n   138\t   `e`, a naming datom's `m` tempid naming its event — and implicit\n   139\t   cardinality-one retractions against the retained history; the\n   140\t   transactor then stamps `t` and appends the resolved datoms as one\n   141\t   atomic transaction record.\n   142\t\n   143\t   Returns `transactor/transact!`'s receipt with prepare-tx's `:tempids`\n   144\t   merged under `:dao.space/tempids`, so a caller keeps the allocated\n   145\t   ids — a name entity is retracted and reasserted through its id, never\n   146\t   a fresh one.\"\n   147\t  [log tx-data]\n   148\t  (let [prepared (transact/prepare-tx\n   149\t                   {:base-datoms (index/snapshot-datoms (:local-stream log))\n   150\t                    :tx-data tx-data})]\n   151\t    (assoc (transactor/transact! log (mapv #(assoc % 3 nil) (:datoms prepared)))\n   152\t           :dao.space/tempids (:tempids prepared))))\n   153\t\n   154\t\n   155\t(defn verify-derivation\n   156\t  \"§5.2.2's verification of a derivation, in two separate steps, before a\n   157\t   segment is trusted.\n   158\t\n   159\t   1. **Content integrity.** The claimed root id is verified against the\n   160\t      root row and every row of the set against its own body — after\n   161\t      `validate-rows`, every row is the reachable closure — and the\n   162\t      vector against the record's `:yin.ledger/output`. A mismatch\n   163\t      reports `{:kind :yin.k/hash-mismatch :value <claimed-address>}`.\n   164\t   2. **Derivation.** Only a consumer implementing the record's profile\n   165\t      exactly recomputes: it re-lowers the row set and the resulting\n   166\t      address must equal `:yin.ledger/output`. Inequality reports\n   167\t      `{:kind :yin.k/derivation-mismatch :output <claimed> :actual\n   168\t      <re-lowered>}` — a defective or dishonest lowering, never repaired\n   169\t      silently. A record under another profile reports `{:kind\n   170\t      :yin.k/profile-mismatch :record <p> :implemented <p>}` and\n   171\t      verifies nothing further; the vector may still load, because step\n   172\t      1 is independent of who lowered it.\n   173\t\n   174\t   `tree` is the projected row set, `vector` the canonical instruction\n   175\t   vector, `record` a `:derive` record map, `implemented` the consumer's\n   176\t   profile (defaults to `lowering-profile`). Returns nil when both steps\n   177\t   pass. A structural defect of tree or vector is reported as the §7.4\n   178\t   or §7.5 defect itself, unchanged: validation names its rule, and a\n   179\t   correct hash is never structural validation.\"\n   180\t  ([tree vector record]\n   181\t   (verify-derivation tree vector record lowering-profile))\n   182\t  ([{:keys [root rows] :as tree} vector record implemented]\n   183\t   (when (not= :derive (:yin.ledger/op record))\n   184\t     (throw (ex-info \"verify-derivation takes a :derive record\"\n   185\t                     {:op (:yin.ledger/op record)})))\n   186\t   (let [row-matches? (fn [id]\n   187\t                        (jing/segment-matches? id (subvec (get rows id) 1)))\n   188\t         hash-mismatch (fn [value] {:kind :yin.k/hash-mismatch :value value})]\n   189\t     (or (vm/validate-rows tree)\n   190\t         (when-not (row-matches? root) (hash-mismatch root))\n   191\t         (some (fn [id] (when-not (row-matches? id) (hash-mismatch id)))\n   192\t               (keys rows))\n   193\t         (code/well-formed-vector? vector)\n   194\t         (let [output (:yin.ledger/output record)]\n   195\t           (when-not (jing/segment-matches? output vector)\n   196\t             (hash-mismatch output)))\n   197\t         (let [profile (:yin.ledger/profile record)]\n   198\t           (when-not (= profile implemented)\n   199\t             {:kind :yin.k/profile-mismatch\n   200\t              :record profile\n   201\t              :implemented implemented}))\n   202\t         (let [output (:yin.ledger/output record)\n   203\t               lowered (:vector (linearize/lower-rows tree))]\n   204\t           (when-not (jing/segment-matches? output lowered)\n   205\t             {:kind :yin.k/derivation-mismatch\n   206\t              :output output\n   207\t              :actual (let [algo (try\n   208\t                                   (jing/segment-algorithm output)\n   209\t                                   (catch #?(:cljd Object\n   210\t                                             :clj Throwable\n   211\t                                             :cljs :default)\n   212\t                                          _\n   213\t                                     jing/default-hash-algorithm))]\n   214\t                        (jing/segment-key lowered {:algorithm algo}))}))))))\n   215\t\n   216\t\n   217\t(defn resolve-name\n   218\t  \"§8.6's current value of a name: the tree address its name entity\n   219\t   refers to under `:yin/code`, through the `current` view — the fold\n   220\t   resolves the greatest `t` per `[e a v]` and removes retractions, so a\n   221\t   retracted-and-reasserted address survives, and a retracted one\n   222\t   resolves to nil. With `t`, the view is as-of bounded: visible datoms\n   223\t   are bounded to `t <= as-of` before folding.\n   224\t\n   225\t   `source` is the refs history as raw d5 rows — what\n   226\t   `dao.space.index/snapshot-datoms` answers over the local stream.\n   227\t   Answers the address, or nil when the name resolves to nothing.\"\n   228\t  ([source sym] (resolve-name source sym nil))\n   229\t  ([source sym t]\n   230\t   (query/collect\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
