{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '360,400p' src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc | nl -ba -v 360\nsed -n '830,870p' src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc | nl -ba -v 830\nsed -n '660,705p' src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc | nl -ba -v 660\"","aggregated_output":"   360\t                        (tuple-defect :argc pc))))\n   361\t                  (range 1 (count t)))))\n   362\t        (range (count v))))\n   363\t\n   364\t\n   365\t(def ^:private vector-rules\n   366\t  [nonempty mnemonic arity operand-kind saturation target-bounds terminator\n   367\t   argc])\n   368\t\n   369\t\n   370\t(defn well-formed-vector?\n   371\t  \"Check one canonical instruction vector (UCF §7.3.2) against §7.5\n   372\t   (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`).\n   373\t\n   374\t   Returns nil when the vector is well formed, else the first defect as\n   375\t   `{:rule r :pc p}` — `:nonempty`, `:mnemonic`, `:arity`,\n   376\t   `:operand-kind`, `:saturation`, `:target-bounds`, `:terminator`, or\n   377\t   `:argc` — with p the offending pc (`:nonempty` names pc 0, where the\n   378\t   missing first instruction belongs). Rules run in §7.5's order, each\n   379\t   assuming the earlier ones held. §2.6 rules 1–4 are satisfied by the\n   380\t   vector form by construction (pc is the index); rules 5–6 and the UCF\n   381\t   §7.3.4 checks are the eight here. The address check — a vector hashing\n   382\t   to the address it claims — is not a structural rule and is not made\n   383\t   here: a correct hash is never structural validation.\"\n   384\t  [v]\n   385\t  (some #(% v) vector-rules))\n   386\t\n   387\t\n   388\t;; =============================================================================\n   389\t;; Segment rows (§6.2)\n   390\t;; =============================================================================\n   391\t\n   392\t(defn project-segment\n   393\t  \"§6.2 (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`): the canonical instruction\n   394\t   vector with pc prepended — one `[pc tag & ops]` row per instruction, the\n   395\t   tag being the mnemonic. The relation is per segment: pcs collide across\n   396\t   segments, and source scope belongs to the interpreter, never to a tuple\n   397\t   slot, so bare-pc rows from different segments are never unioned into one\n   398\t   relation. A query over several segments takes them as separate sources\n   399\t   or uses the segment-qualified form (`project-segment-qualified`).\"\n   400\t  [v]\n   830\t  (let [{:keys [owner conflict]} (all-body-chains v)]\n   831\t    (if conflict\n   832\t      (tuple-defect :scope-conflict conflict)\n   833\t      (some (fn [pc]\n   834\t              (let [t (nth v pc)]\n   835\t                (when (= :load-bound (mnemonic-of t))\n   836\t                  (let [depth (nth t 1), position (nth t 2), chain (get owner pc)]\n   837\t                    (when (or (nil? chain)\n   838\t                              (not (nonneg-int? depth))\n   839\t                              (not (nonneg-int? position))\n   840\t                              (>= depth (count chain))\n   841\t                              (>= position (nth chain depth)))\n   842\t                      (tuple-defect :scope pc))))))\n   843\t            (range (count v))))))\n   844\t\n   845\t\n   846\t(defn image-defect\n   847\t  \"The B1 image validator (S2, S3): the first defect in `v`, checking\n   848\t   generic shape first, then the scope rule S3 adds. nil when `v` is a\n   849\t   valid executable image on this path -- the only admitter of one; no\n   850\t   projected reader participates.\"\n   851\t  [v]\n   852\t  (or (well-formed-image? v) (scope-defect v)))\n   660\t   past a malformed body's own end), so a body whose terminator is wrong\n   661\t   must be rejected by `terminator-rule` first rather than crash inside\n   662\t   the dataflow.\"\n   663\t  [body-scope-rule jump-scope-rule register-bounds-rule terminator-rule\n   664\t   live-shape-rule live-bounds-rule live-tail-rule live-exact-rule])\n   665\t\n   666\t\n   667\t(defn register-image-defect\n   668\t  \"The sole admitter of a `{:bodies [...], :instructions [...]}` register\n   669\t   image on this path: nil when it is well formed, else the first defect\n   670\t   -- a tuple defect `{:rule r :pc p}` or a body defect `{:rule r :body\n   671\t   i}`. Structural rules run first (they alone make `:bodies`/pc-indexed\n   672\t   access into `instructions` safe for the body rules that follow).\"\n   673\t  [image]\n   674\t  (or (some #(% image) structural-rules)\n   675\t      (some #(% image) body-rules)))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
