{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '90,185p' src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc | nl -ba -v 90\nsed -n '95,190p' src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc | nl -ba -v 95\nsed -n '715,755p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc | nl -ba -v 715\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '770,865p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | nl -ba -v 770\"","aggregated_output":"   770\t## §5 The segment level\n   771\t\n   772\t### 5.1 The instruction vector is UCF §7.3.2\n   773\t\n   774\tThe segment's canonical form is the positional instruction tuple vector of\n   775\tUCF §7.3.2: one tuple per instruction, pc is the index, the\n   776\t`yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4 table fixes each mnemonic's arity and operand\n   777\tkinds, defaults are saturated, refs are resolved pcs, and the header folds\n   778\taway. Its address is `(dao.jing/segment-key vector)`, the value\n   779\t`yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.2 already types `:yin.code/hash` as. The two\n   780\tlevels are uniform under this design: an AST row (§2.1) and an instruction\n   781\ttuple are both flat positional tuples, canonical and content-addressed,\n   782\tand the instruction vector was already this shape before the flat-row\n   783\truling clarified the AST level. To perfectly preserve program semantics across boundaries, both the Universal Map AST and the Semantic Tuples strictly retain named variables. Therefore, `\"v2\"` everywhere in this document means the Named Variable grammar. This design otherwise adds\n   784\tnothing to that form and restates none of it.\n   785\t\n   786\t### 5.2 The hash chain is realized by derivation records\n   787\t\n   788\t```\n   789\ttree-address ──lower under lowering profile L, targeting execution contract C──▶ segment-address\n   790\t```\n   791\t\n   792\tNeither value carries the other's address: the tree is the input, the\n   793\tvector excludes `:yin.code/derived-from` (UCF §7.3.2, *The header folds\n   794\taway*). The link is a **derivation record**, itself content-addressed\n   795\t(§8.2):\n   796\t\n   797\t```clojure\n   798\t{:yin.ledger/op       :derive\n   799\t :yin.ledger/input    <tree-address>\n   800\t :yin.ledger/output   <segment-address>\n   801\t :yin.ledger/function :yin.vm/lower\n   802\t :yin.ledger/profile  {:yin.lower/profile \"ast-to-bytecode\"          ; the lowering profile, §5.2.1\n   803\t                       :yin.code/contract \"v2\"              ; the UCF §7.3.3 execution contract it targets\n   804\t                       :yin.k/version     0}}\n   805\t```\n   806\t\n   807\tIts address is published in the ledger (§8.3). The chain\n   808\t`record → input, record → output` is what \"the hash chain is the ledger\"\n   809\tmeans under this design: the chain lives in ledger records and refs, not\n   810\tin the code content, which rightly excludes it.\n   811\t\n   812\t#### 5.2.1 The lowering profile\n   813\t\n   814\tUCF §7.3.3's execution-contract stamp versions the instruction grammar,\n   815\tthe transitions, resolution precedence, effect outcomes, and scheduling.\n   816\tIts declared scope does not include the AST-to-instruction algorithm, and\n   817\ttwo deterministic lowerers can target one execution contract and emit two\n   818\tdifferent, equally valid vectors. A derivation is therefore verifiable\n   819\tonly against a **lowering profile**, a published revision that pins:\n   820\t\n   821\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   822\t| Pinned by the profile                 | Reference value for `\"ast-to-bytecode\"`                                                                                                 |\n   823\t+=======================================+================================================================================================================================+\n   824\t| input grammar                         | the §2 tuple grammar, by revision of this document                                                                             |\n   825\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   826\t| normalization applied before emission | saturation (§2.4), tail marks on `:application` only (§2.3), nothing else                                                      |\n   827\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   828\t| emission rules                        | the §5.3 flattening table of `yin.vm.semantic.md`, in the walker's evaluation order (operator, then operands left to right)    |\n   829\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   830\t| label and body order                  | labels numbered in emission order (`linearize.cljc:76-77`); bodies emitted after the main sequence, in the order lambdas were  |\n   831\t|                                       | encountered, including lambdas found inside bodies (`linearize.cljc:151-158`)                                                  |\n   832\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   833\t| terminators                           | main sequence ends in `:halt`, every body in `:return` (`linearize.cljc:150`, `:157`)                                          |\n   834\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   835\t| target execution contract             | the UCF §7.3.3 stamp the emitted grammar must satisfy                                                                          |\n   836\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   837\t\n   838\tThe profile is a dedicated stamp, not an extension of the execution\n   839\tcontract: an execution contract may be targeted by several profiles, and a\n   840\tprofile is revised whenever any pinned rule changes, whether or not the\n   841\texecution contract does. A lowering that introduces any host-dependent\n   842\tchoice violates its profile and is a defect. The profile is the structured\n   843\tmap above, carried **exactly** in the record and in the event projection\n   844\t(§8.2); no string form of it is defined and none is used.\n   845\t\n   846\t#### 5.2.2 Verification, in two separate steps\n   847\t\n   848\tA consumer holding a tree, a vector, and a derivation record verifies two\n   849\tdifferent things and reports them separately:\n   850\t\n   851\t1. **Content integrity.** The consumer verifies the claimed root ID against the root row and verifies each required row as `row.id == segment-key(row-body)`, recursively validating the reachable closure before reconstructing the semantic map. The instruction vector is verified similarly. Failure is\n   852\t   `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the value. This is UCF §7.3.4's check and\n   853\t   says nothing about the derivation.\n   854\t2. **Derivation.** If, and only if, the consumer implements the record's\n   855\t   `:yin.lower/profile` exactly, it lowers the reconstructed map under that profile and verifies the resulting instruction-vector address against `:yin.ledger/output`. Equality\n   856\t   verifies the asserted derivation; inequality is `:yin.k/derivation-\n   857\t   mismatch`, a defective or dishonest lowering, never repaired silently. A\n   858\t   consumer implementing a different profile does not recompute: it reports\n   859\t   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` naming both profiles, the same outcome UCF\n   860\t   §7.3.3 gives a different execution contract, and it may still *load* the\n   861\t   output vector if the vector's own execution contract is one it\n   862\t   implements, because content integrity (step 1) is independent of who\n   863\t   lowered it.\n   864\t\n   865\tTwo trees that lower to one vector under one profile yield two records\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '90,185p' src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc | nl -ba -v 90\nsed -n '95,190p' src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc | nl -ba -v 95\nsed -n '715,755p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc | nl -ba -v 715\"","aggregated_output":"    90\t(def format-tag\n    91\t  \"The model tag every register payload carries as `:format`.\"\n    92\t  :yin.debruijn.code)\n    93\t\n    94\t\n    95\t(defn load-image\n    96\t  \"Load `segment` (a vector of instructions) into `vm` as its one image:\n    97\t   H is recomputed, the registers are reset to pc 0 with empty frames,\n    98\t   stack, and continuation, and the machine is running unless the segment\n    99\t   is empty. The store, the parked map, the wait set, the ready queue,\n   100\t   the id counter, and the composition values survive, exactly as they\n   101\t   survive a `yin.vm.semantic/load-vector`. A continuation parked under an\n   102\t   earlier image cannot be restored against this one: `stack-restore`\n   103\t   refuses it by H.\"\n   104\t  [vm segment]\n   105\t  (assoc vm\n   106\t         :segment segment\n   107\t         :hash (dcode/image-hash segment)\n   108\t         :pc 0\n   109\t         :frames []\n   110\t         :stack []\n   111\t         :continuation []\n   112\t         :halted? (empty? segment)\n   113\t         :blocked? false\n   114\t         :value nil))\n   115\t\n   116\t\n   117\t(defn create-vm\n   118\t  \"Build a fresh `DebruijnVM` over `segment` (a vector of instructions).\n   119\t\n   120\t   Options: `:free-env`, `:store`, `:primitives`, `:modules` (all default to\n   121\t   `{}`, matching the named VM's empty defaults except that `:primitives`\n   122\t   defaults to `{}` here whereas `yin.vm/empty-state` populates the full\n   123\t   primitive table; callers pass `yin.vm/primitives` when they want the\n   124\t   standard registry), `:make-stream` (the host's stream constructor; no\n   125\t   for every v2 VM), `:call-in`/`:call-out`/`:call-capacity` (the FFI pair,\n   126\t   built by `yin.vm/empty-state` exactly as the semantic VM's is), and\n   127\t   `:bridge` (host FFI handlers, attached by `yin.vm.ffi/attach`).\n   128\t\n   129\t   An empty segment starts halted with an empty program, as the semantic\n   130\t   VM's `create-vm` does; `load-image` loads work into it.\n   131\t\n   132\t   Always fresh: never resume or mutate a VM that has already run, per the\n   133\t   design's D4 fixture restriction (the named VM's environment-leak defect\n   134\t   is out of scope here and must not be reproduced by accident).\"\n   135\t  ([segment] (create-vm segment {}))\n   136\t  ([segment opts]\n   137\t   (let [base (vm/empty-state\n   138\t                (assoc (select-keys opts [:modules :make-stream :call-in\n   139\t                                          :call-out :call-capacity])\n   140\t                       :primitives (or (:primitives opts) {})))]\n   141\t     (-> (map->DebruijnVM\n   142\t           {:segment [],\n   143\t            :hash nil,\n   144\t            :pc 0,\n   145\t            :frames [],\n   146\t            :free-env (or (:free-env opts) {}),\n   147\t            :stack [],\n   148\t            :continuation [],\n   149\t            :store (merge (:store base) (:store opts)),\n   150\t            :blocked? false,\n   151\t            :halted? true,\n   152\t            :wait-set [],\n   153\t            :ready-queue [],\n   154\t            :parked {},\n   155\t            :id-counter 0,\n   156\t            :value nil,\n   157\t            :make-stream (:make-stream base),\n   158\t            :bridge nil,\n   159\t            :primitives (:primitives base),\n   160\t            :modules (or (:modules opts) {})})\n   161\t         (load-image segment)\n   162\t         (ffi/attach (:bridge opts))))))\n   163\t\n   164\t\n   165\t;; =============================================================================\n   166\t;; Frame addressing\n   167\t;; =============================================================================\n   168\t\n   169\t(defn- frame-value\n   170\t  \"Section 4: frame 0 for `:load-bound` is the INNERMOST frame, read from\n   171\t   the END of `:frames` -- the opposite of a naive front-of-vector reading,\n   172\t   and deliberate per the design. `frames` is outermost-first, so depth `d`\n   173\t   is `(count frames) - 1 - d`.\"\n   174\t  [frames depth position]\n   175\t  (let [n (count frames)\n   176\t        idx (- n 1 depth)]\n   177\t    (when (or (neg? idx) (>= idx n))\n   178\t      (throw (ex-info \"load-bound: depth out of range\"\n   179\t                      {:rule :load-bound-depth, :depth depth,\n   180\t                       :frame-count n})))\n   181\t    (let [frame (nth frames idx)]\n   182\t      (when (or (neg? position) (>= position (count frame)))\n   183\t        (throw (ex-info \"load-bound: position out of range\"\n   184\t                        {:rule :load-bound-position, :depth depth,\n   185\t                         :position position, :frame-arity (count frame)})))\n    95\t(defn- bind-positional\n    96\t  \"Positional parameter binding: missing arguments are nil-filled,\n    97\t   extra arguments dropped.\"\n    98\t  [arity args]\n    99\t  (vec (take arity (concat args (repeat nil)))))\n   100\t\n   101\t\n   102\t;; =============================================================================\n   103\t;; Image loading & VM construction\n   104\t;; =============================================================================\n   105\t\n   106\t(defn load-image\n   107\t  \"Load `segment` (a register image `{:bodies [...], :instructions [...]}`)\n   108\t   into `vm` as its one image: R is computed, the registers are reset to\n   109\t   pc 0 with empty frames and continuation, and the machine is running\n   110\t   unless the segment is empty. A non-empty image must pass the register\n   111\t   validator. A continuation parked under an earlier image cannot be\n   112\t   restored against this one: `register-restore` refuses it by R.\"\n   113\t  [vm segment]\n   114\t  (let [empty-seg? (or (nil? segment)\n   115\t                       (empty? segment)\n   116\t                       (and (map? segment)\n   117\t                            (empty? (:instructions segment))))]\n   118\t    (if empty-seg?\n   119\t      (assoc vm\n   120\t             :segment {:bodies [], :instructions []}\n   121\t             :hash nil\n   122\t             :pc 0\n   123\t             :frames []\n   124\t             :registers []\n   125\t             :continuation []\n   126\t             :halted? true\n   127\t             :blocked? false\n   128\t             :value nil)\n   129\t      (if-let [defect (rcode/register-image-defect segment)]\n   130\t        (throw (ex-info (str \"Invalid register image: \" (:rule defect))\n   131\t                        defect))\n   132\t        (let [body0 (first (:bodies segment))\n   133\t              reg-count (or (:registers body0) 0)]\n   134\t          (assoc vm\n   135\t                 :segment segment\n   136\t                 :hash (rcode/register-hash segment)\n   137\t                 :pc 0\n   138\t                 :frames []\n   139\t                 :registers (vec (repeat reg-count nil))\n   140\t                 :continuation []\n   141\t                 :halted? false\n   142\t                 :blocked? false\n   143\t                 :value nil))))))\n   144\t\n   145\t\n   146\t(defn create-vm\n   147\t  \"Build a fresh `DebruijnRegisterVM` over `segment`.\n   148\t   Options: `:free-env`, `:store`, `:primitives`, `:modules`, `:make-stream`,\n   149\t   `:call-in`/`:call-out`/`:call-capacity`, and `:bridge`.\"\n   150\t  ([segment] (create-vm segment {}))\n   151\t  ([segment opts]\n   152\t   (let [base (vm/empty-state\n   153\t                (assoc (select-keys opts [:modules :make-stream :call-in\n   154\t                                          :call-out :call-capacity])\n   155\t                       :primitives (or (:primitives opts) {})))]\n   156\t     (-> (map->DebruijnRegisterVM\n   157\t           {:segment {:bodies [], :instructions []},\n   158\t            :hash nil,\n   159\t            :pc 0,\n   160\t            :frames [],\n   161\t            :free-env (or (:free-env opts) {}),\n   162\t            :registers [],\n   163\t            :continuation [],\n   164\t            :store (merge (:store base) (:store opts)),\n   165\t            :blocked? false,\n   166\t            :halted? true,\n   167\t            :wait-set [],\n   168\t            :ready-queue [],\n   169\t            :parked {},\n   170\t            :id-counter 0,\n   171\t            :value nil,\n   172\t            :make-stream (:make-stream base),\n   173\t            :bridge nil,\n   174\t            :primitives (:primitives base),\n   175\t            :modules (or (:modules opts) {})})\n   176\t         (load-image segment)\n   177\t         (ffi/attach (:bridge opts))))))\n   178\t\n   179\t\n   180\t;; =============================================================================\n   181\t;; Return transitions & restore\n   182\t;; =============================================================================\n   183\t\n   184\t(defn- return-transition\n   185\t  \"Unified return transition: restores caller frame from continuation if\n   186\t   pending, otherwise halts the VM with `val`.\"\n   187\t  [vm val]\n   188\t  (let [continuation (:continuation vm)]\n   189\t    (if (seq continuation)\n   190\t      (let [frame (peek continuation)\n   715\t                     (throw (ex-info \"Mnemonic has no opcode\" {:op mnem})))]\n   716\t      (into [opcode] (rest t)))))\n   717\t\n   718\t\n   719\t(defn load-vector\n   720\t  \"Load one canonical instruction vector (UCF §7.3.2) by the direct path\n   721\t   (§7.1): validate it against §7.5 — a defect throws naming the pc — then\n   722\t   decode the positional operands into the same image `load-image` builds\n   723\t   from the projected batch, and register it under `:code` with the\n   724\t   vector's address `(jing/segment-key v)` in the alias column.\n   725\t\n   726\t   A vector claims no local id, so one is minted below the loaded floor\n   727\t   (`vm/loaded-code-floor`), as `ast-loader` mints for lowered AST batches;\n   728\t   an address already aliased reloads under its id, which `store-image`\n   729\t   accepts as the identical image. Options:\n   730\t     :id claim this local segment id instead of minting one. The §3.1 rule\n   731\t          applies: an id already holding a different image is a load error,\n   732\t          an identical reload is accepted. Ignored when the address is\n   733\t          already aliased.\"\n   734\t  ([vm v] (load-vector vm v {}))\n   735\t  ([vm v opts]\n   736\t   (when-let [defect (code/well-formed-vector? v)]\n   737\t     (throw (ex-info (str \"Cannot load vector: \" (name (:rule defect))\n   738\t                          \" (pc \" (:pc defect) \")\")\n   739\t                     {:defect defect})))\n   740\t   (let [address (jing/segment-key v)\n   741\t         aliases (:code-aliases vm)\n   742\t         seg (or (get aliases address)\n   743\t                 (:id opts)\n   744\t                 (dec (vm/loaded-code-floor (:code vm))))\n   745\t         image {:segment seg,\n   746\t                :length (count v),\n   747\t                :code (mapv decode-tuple v),\n   748\t                :address address}]\n   749\t     (assoc vm\n   750\t            :program seg\n   751\t            :code (store-image (:code vm) image)\n   752\t            :code-aliases (store-alias aliases image)\n   753\t            :control {:segment seg, :pc 0}\n   754\t            :stack []\n   755\t            :k nil\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
